Yes, the study must address both facility siting and human factors, and not only equipment failures [1][2][3]. 

When conducting a process hazard analysis such as a Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP), the study must address all of the following [1][2][3]:
- The hazards of the process [1][2][3];
- The identification of any previous incident that had a likely potential for catastrophic consequences in the workplace [1][2][3];
- Engineering and administrative controls applicable to the hazards and their interrelationships [1][2][3];
- Consequences of failure of engineering and administrative controls [1][2][3];
- Facility siting [1][2][3];
- Human factors [1][2][3]; and
- A qualitative evaluation of a range of the possible safety and health effects on employees in the workplace resulting from a failure of controls [1][2][3].

Additionally, a PHA is directed to focus not just on equipment, but also on instrumentation, utilities, routine and nonroutine human actions, and external factors that might impact the process [5].

Yes, when conducting a HAZOP (or any process hazard analysis methodology) on a covered process, the study must address facility siting and human factors, rather than focusing only on equipment failures [1][2][3][5]. 

Regardless of the methodology selected, a process hazard analysis must address all of the following:
- Hazards of the process [1][2][3][5];
- Identification of any previous incident with a likely potential for catastrophic consequences in the workplace [1][2][3][5];
- Applicable engineering and administrative controls and their interrelationships [1][2][3];
- Consequences of failure of engineering and administrative controls [1][2][3];
- Facility siting [1][2][3];
- Human factors [1][2][3];
- A qualitative evaluation of a range of possible safety and health effects on employees if there is a failure of controls [1][2][3].

Furthermore, a PHA is required to focus not just on equipment, but also on instrumentation, utilities, human actions (routine and nonroutine), and external factors that could impact the process [4].
